dequately to unexpected events (maybe). Technical competence: solves problems extremely well, concentration, but no explicit weather or systems. Decision quality? Not explicit. Crew cooperation: arrogant, weak leadership (negative). Professional communication: hot temper (negative). Adaptability: strong (responds to unexpected). Might be problematic due to temperament.\n\nCandidate A: reliability? Anticipate dangerous situations, leadership, spatial vision (good). Stress resilience? Not explicit. Technical competence: see complex connections, spatial vision (good). Decision quality? Anticipation. Crew cooperation: leadership but doesn't take criticism, show-off, not open to new ideas (negative). Professional communication: not open to new ideas, show-off (negative). Adaptability: not open to new ideas (low). Overall concerns.\n\nThus candidate B still strong on reliability and stress, but crew cooperation and communication issues are serious. Candidate C has strong crew cooperation and calmness, but adaptability low and communication difficulty. Which is more critical? For long-distance flights, crew cooperation and communication are vital. Also adaptability and willingness to develop are important. Candidate B's nasty remarks could be safety risk. Candidate C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education also risk.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate overall best fits. Perhaps Candidate C's positive crew atmosphere outweighs B's communication negativity? But C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education may affect long-term development. However B's memory weakness for numbers could affect safety (numeric data). That is concerning.\n\nGiven the panel, we might shift recommendation to Candidate C, emphasizing crew cooperation and calmness, decision quality, and technical competence, while acknowledging adaptability concerns but perhaps less severe than B's communication hostility.\n\nWe should reference Agent 1's crew cooperation evidence and our own n